\end{center}

Three trained classifiers predict the class of a new patient as: $M_1=$ Sick, $M_2=$ Sick, $M_3=$ Healthy. Combine these using majority vote.

\textbf{Solution:}
Count the votes for each class.
\begin{align*}
\text{Votes for Sick} &= 2 \\
\text{Votes for Healthy} &= 1
\end{align*}
Sick has more votes.
\boxed{\text{Final prediction: Sick}}

Five trained classifiers predict the class of a new email as: Spam, NotSpam, Spam, Spam, NotSpam. Combine these using majority vote.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
\text{Votes for Spam} &= 3 \\
\text{Votes for NotSpam} &= 2
\end{align*}
Spam has more votes.
\boxed{\text{Final prediction: Spam}}
An \textbf{odd} number of classifiers is often chosen for a two-class problem, for the same reason an odd $k$ is preferred in k-NN (Section 3.4): it prevents an exact tie in the vote.

Three regression models predict the price of a house (in lakhs) as: $M_1=50$, $M_2=54$, $M_3=52$. Combine these using averaging.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
\text{Combined prediction} &= \frac{M_1+M_2+M_3}{3} \\
&= \frac{50+54+52}{3} = \frac{156}{3}
\end{align*}
\boxed{\text{Final prediction} = 52 \text{ lakhs}}

\textbf{Combining Rules:}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item \textbf{Classification:} combine by \textbf{majority vote} (the class chosen by the most base learners), as in Examples 5.1 and 5.2
\item \textbf{Regression:} combine by \textbf{averaging} the numeric predictions, as in Example 5.3
\item Every base learner contributes equally in a simple vote or average; some advanced ensembles instead use a \textbf{weighted} vote, giving more trusted learners more influence
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Why Ensembles Work}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item Each base learner must be \textbf{better than random guessing} on its own \hfill\textit{(accuracy $p>0.5$ for a two-class problem)}
\item The base learners' mistakes should be as \textbf{independent (diverse)} as possible \hfill\textit{(errors should not all happen on the same examples)}
\item If both conditions hold, combining by majority vote gives \textbf{higher} accuracy than any single base learner
\end{enumerate}

This connects directly to probability: if each of 3 independent classifiers is correct with probability $p$, the ensemble is correct whenever a \textbf{majority} (2 or more) is correct, which is a binomial probability calculation.

Three independent classifiers each have accuracy $p=0.7$. Assuming their errors are independent, compute the accuracy of the majority-vote ensemble.

\textbf{Solution:}
The ensemble is correct if all 3 are correct, or exactly 2 out of 3 are correct.
\begin{align*}
P(\text{all 3 correct}) &= p^3 = (0.7)^3 = 0.343 \\
P(\text{exactly 2 correct}) &= \binom{3}{2}p^2(1-p) = 3(0.49)(0.3) = 0.441 \\[4pt]
P(\text{ensemble correct}) &= 0.343+0.441
\end{align*}
\boxed{P(\text{ensemble correct}) = 0.784}
The ensemble's accuracy ($78.4\%$) is higher than any single classifier's accuracy ($70\%$).

Three independent classifiers each have accuracy $p=0.6$. Compute the accuracy of the majority-vote ensemble.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
P(\text{all 3 correct}) &= (0.6)^3 = 0.216 \\
P(\text{exactly 2 correct}) &= \binom{3}{2}(0.6)^2(0.4) = 3(0.36)(0.4) = 0.432 \\[4pt]
P(\text{ensemble correct}) &= 0.216+0.432
\end{align*}
\boxed{P(\text{ensemble correct}) = 0.648}
The improvement is smaller here ($60\%\to64.8\%$) than in Example 5.4 ($70\%\to78.4\%$), showing that stronger base learners give the ensemble more to work with.

Three independent classifiers each have accuracy $p=0.4$ (worse than random guessing). Compute the accuracy of the majority-vote ensemble.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
P(\text{all 3 correct}) &= (0.4)^3 = 0.064 \\
P(\text{exactly 2 correct}) &= \binom{3}{2}(0.4)^2(0.6) = 3(0.16)(0.6) = 0.288 \\[4pt]
P(\text{ensemble correct}) &= 0.064+0.288
\end{align*}
\boxed{P(\text{ensemble correct}) = 0.352}
Here the ensemble accuracy ($35.2\%$) is even \textbf{worse} than a single classifier's accuracy ($40\%$). This confirms Property 1 of the theorembox above: if the base learners are not better than random guessing, combining them does not help and can make things worse. \checkmark

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
    width=8.5cm,height=5.5cm,
    xlabel={Base Learner Accuracy $p$},
    ylabel={Ensemble Accuracy},
    xmin=0.3,xmax=0.8,ymin=0.2,ymax=0.9,
    axis lines=left,
    ymajorgrids=true,
]
\addplot[primaryblue,thick,mark=*,mark size=1.8pt] coordinates {(0.4,0.352)(0.6,0.648)(0.7,0.784)};
\addplot[darkgray,dashed,thick,domain=0.3:0.8,samples=2] {x};
\node[darkgray,font=\scriptsize] at (axis cs:0.75,0.68) {$y=p$ (no ensemble)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

An original training set has 5 examples: $X_1,X_2,X_3,X_4,X_5$. A bootstrap sample is drawn and turns out to be $X_2,X_2,X_4,X_1,X_4$. Identify which original examples are repeated and which are left out.

\textbf{Solution:}
Count how many times each original example appears in the bootstrap sample.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cc}
\toprule
Example & Times Selected \\
\midrule
$X_1$ & 1 \\
$X_2$ & 2 \\
$X_3$ & 0 \\
$X_4$ & 2 \\
$X_5$ & 0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\boxed{\text{Repeated: } X_2, X_4 \qquad \text{Left out: } X_3, X_5}

An original training set has $n=4$ examples. One example, $X_1$, is drawn from this set 4 times, with replacement, to build one bootstrap sample. Compute the probability that $X_1$ is never selected.

\textbf{Solution:}
Each single draw avoids $X_1$ with probability $\dfrac{n-1}{n}=\dfrac{3}{4}$, and the 4 draws are independent.
\begin{align*}
P(X_1 \text{ never selected}) &= \left(\frac{3}{4}\right)^4 \\
&= \frac{81}{256}
\end{align*}
\boxed{P \approx 0.316}

Repeat Example 5.8 for an original training set of size $n=5$, drawn 5 times with replacement.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
P(X_1 \text{ never selected}) &= \left(\frac{4}{5}\right)^5 \\
&= \frac{1024}{3125}
\end{align*}
\boxed{P \approx 0.328}
Comparing Examples 5.8 and 5.9, the probability is rising slowly toward a fixed value as $n$ grows. In fact $\left(1-\frac{1}{n}\right)^n \to e^{-1} \approx 0.368$ as $n$ becomes large.

\textbf{The 63.2 / 36.8 Rule:}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item As $n$ grows large, about $63.2\%$ of the original examples appear at least once in a bootstrap sample
\item The remaining $36.8\%$ are left out of that particular bootstrap sample entirely
\item These left-out examples are called \textbf{out-of-bag (OOB)} examples for that sample, and are used again in Section 5.2.2
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{The Bagging Algorithm}

Bagging (short for Bootstrap Aggregating) trains $B$ base learners independently, each on its own separate bootstrap sample drawn from the same original training set. The $B$ trained models are then combined exactly as in Section 5.1: by \textbf{majority vote} for classification, or by \textbf{averaging} for regression.

