Five decision trees, each trained on a different bootstrap sample, predict the class of a new patient as: Sick, Sick, Healthy, Sick, Healthy. Give the bagging prediction.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
\text{Votes for Sick} &= 3 \\
\text{Votes for Healthy} &= 2
\end{align*}
Sick has the majority.
\boxed{\text{Bagging prediction: Sick}}

Four regression trees, each trained on a different bootstrap sample, predict a house price (lakhs) as: 48, 52, 50, 54. Give the bagging prediction.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
\text{Combined prediction} &= \frac{48+52+50+54}{4} \\
&= \frac{204}{4}
\end{align*}
\boxed{52 \text{ lakhs}}

An original training set has 6 examples $X_1,\ldots,X_6$. One bootstrap sample drawn for model $M_1$ is: $X_3,X_1,X_3,X_6,X_1,X_3$. List the out-of-bag examples for $M_1$.

\textbf{Solution:}
Selected examples: $X_1$ (yes), $X_2$ (no), $X_3$ (yes), $X_4$ (no), $X_5$ (no), $X_6$ (yes).
\boxed{\text{OOB for } M_1: X_2, X_4, X_5}
These 3 out-of-bag points can be used to test $M_1$'s accuracy without needing a separate validation set, since $M_1$ never saw them during training.

Boosting trains base learners \textbf{sequentially}, one after another, rather than independently in parallel like bagging. Each new base learner is trained to pay \textbf{more attention} to the examples that previous learners got wrong, by increasing the weight of misclassified examples. The final prediction is a \textbf{weighted} vote of all the base learners, where more accurate learners get a bigger say.

Unlike bagging, where every bootstrap sample is drawn independently and every model counts equally, boosting builds each model specifically to fix the previous model's mistakes, and trusts more accurate models more when voting.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={font=\scriptsize},
  model/.style={rectangle,rounded corners,draw=primaryblue,thick,fill=primaryblue!8,minimum width=1.8cm,minimum height=0.8cm,align=center},
  arr/.style={draw,thick,-{Stealth[length=2.5mm]}}
]
\node[model] (m1) at (0,0) {$M_1$ trained\\ on original\\ weights};
\node[model] (m2) at (4,0) {$M_2$ trained,\\ weights raised\\ on $M_1$'s errors};
\node[model] (m3) at (8,0) {$M_3$ trained,\\ weights raised\\ on $M_2$'s errors};
\draw[arr] (m1) -- node[above,font=\scriptsize]{reweight} (m2);
\draw[arr] (m2) -- node[above,font=\scriptsize]{reweight} (m3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\subsection{Example Weights and Model Weight (AdaBoost-Style)}

A base learner $M_1$ has weighted error rate $\epsilon_1=0.2$. Compute its voting weight $\alpha_1$. (Use $\ln 4 \approx 1.386$.)

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
\alpha_1 &= \frac{1}{2}\ln\left(\frac{1-0.2}{0.2}\right) = \frac{1}{2}\ln\left(\frac{0.8}{0.2}\right) = \frac{1}{2}\ln(4)
\end{align*}
\boxed{\alpha_1 \approx 0.693}

A base learner $M_2$ has weighted error rate $\epsilon_2=0.4$. Compute its voting weight $\alpha_2$. (Use $\ln 1.5 \approx 0.405$.)

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
\alpha_2 &= \frac{1}{2}\ln\left(\frac{1-0.4}{0.4}\right) = \frac{1}{2}\ln\left(\frac{0.6}{0.4}\right) = \frac{1}{2}\ln(1.5)
\end{align*}
\boxed{\alpha_2 \approx 0.203}
Comparing to Example 5.13: $M_1$ (error $0.2$) receives a much higher voting weight $\alpha_1\approx0.693$ than $M_2$ (error $0.4$) with $\alpha_2\approx0.203$, exactly as expected from the definition — the more accurate learner earns more influence.

Three trained learners predict the class of a new example as: $M_1=+1$ (with $\alpha_1=0.8$), $M_2=-1$ (with $\alpha_2=0.5$), $M_3=+1$ (with $\alpha_3=0.3$). Compute the final weighted-vote prediction.

\textbf{Solution:}
Sum the voting weights separately for each predicted class.
\begin{align*}
\text{Weight for } +1 &= \alpha_1+\alpha_3 = 0.8+0.3 = 1.1 \\
\text{Weight for } -1 &= \alpha_2 = 0.5
\end{align*}
Since $1.1 > 0.5$:
\boxed{\text{Final prediction: } +1}

\textbf{Bagging vs Boosting:}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{2.6cm}p{4.6cm}p{4.6cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Bagging} & \textbf{Boosting} \\
\midrule
Training order & Parallel, independent & Sequential, each depends on the last \\
Sampling & Bootstrap (random, with replacement) & Full data, but example weights change each round \\
Combining & Simple majority vote / average & Weighted vote using each $\alpha_t$ \\
Main goal & Reduce variance & Reduce bias (and variance) \\
Best base learner & High-variance (deep trees) & Weak learner (shallow trees, "stumps") \\
Risk & Less prone to overfitting & Can overfit if run for too many rounds \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Four training examples start with equal weight $w_i=0.25$. Base learner $M_1$ misclassifies $X_2$ and $X_4$, and correctly classifies $X_1$ and $X_3$. If misclassified examples have their weight doubled and correctly classified examples have their weight halved, find the new weights before renormalization, and then the renormalized weights.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
w_1' &= 0.25\times0.5 = 0.125 \ (\text{correct}) \\
w_2' &= 0.25\times2 = 0.5 \ (\text{misclassified}) \\
w_3' &= 0.25\times0.5 = 0.125 \ (\text{correct}) \\
w_4' &= 0.25\times2 = 0.5 \ (\text{misclassified}) \\[4pt]
\text{Sum} &= 0.125+0.5+0.125+0.5 = 1.25
\end{align*}
Renormalize by dividing each weight by the sum $1.25$:
\begin{align*}
w_1'' &= \frac{0.125}{1.25}=0.1, \quad w_2'' = \frac{0.5}{1.25}=0.4, \quad w_3'' = \frac{0.125}{1.25}=0.1, \quad w_4'' = \frac{0.5}{1.25}=0.4
\end{align*}
\boxed{\text{New weights: } w_1=0.1,\ w_2=0.4,\ w_3=0.1,\ w_4=0.4}
$X_2$ and $X_4$ (misclassified) now carry far more weight ($0.4$ each) than $X_1$ and $X_3$ ($0.1$ each), so the next base learner $M_2$ will be trained to focus much more heavily on getting $X_2$ and $X_4$ correct.
